# Jenkins Setup on Ubuntu

This document explains how to install and configure Jenkins on an Ubuntu EC2 server.

## 1. Update Ubuntu

Update the package list:

```bash
sudo apt update
```

Upgrade installed packages:

```bash
sudo apt upgrade -y
```

---

## 2. Install Java

Jenkins requires Java.

Install OpenJDK 21:

```bash
sudo apt install fontconfig openjdk-21-jre -y
```

Verify Java:

```bash
java -version
```

Expected output:

```text
openjdk version "21.x.x"
```

---

## 3. Add Jenkins Repository

Create the Jenkins keyring:

```bash
sudo mkdir -p /etc/apt/keyrings
```

Download the Jenkins signing key:

```bash
sudo wget -O /etc/apt/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc \
  https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable/jenkins.io-2026.key
```

Add the Jenkins repository:

```bash
echo "deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/jenkins-keyring.asc] https://pkg.jenkins.io/debian-stable binary/" | sudo tee \
  /etc/apt/sources.list.d/jenkins.list > /dev/null
```

Update the package list:

```bash
sudo apt update
```

---

## 4. Install Jenkins

Install Jenkins:

```bash
sudo apt install jenkins -y
```

Start Jenkins:

```bash
sudo systemctl start jenkins
```

Enable Jenkins to start automatically after reboot:

```bash
sudo systemctl enable jenkins
```

Check Jenkins status:

```bash
sudo systemctl status jenkins
```

Expected:

```text
Active: active (running)
```

---

## 5. Allow Jenkins Port

Jenkins normally runs on port `8080`.

If UFW is enabled:

```bash
sudo ufw allow 8080/tcp
```

Check firewall rules:

```bash
sudo ufw status
```

Also make sure **TCP port 8080** is allowed in the AWS EC2 Security Group.

For production environments, it is better to access Jenkins through **Nginx + HTTPS** instead of exposing port 8080 directly to the internet.

---

## 6. Open Jenkins

Find the EC2 public IP:

```bash
curl ifconfig.me
```

Open in the browser:

```text
http://YOUR_EC2_PUBLIC_IP:8080
```

Example:

```text
http://13.234.100.20:8080
```

---

## 7. Get Initial Jenkins Password

Jenkins provides an initial administrator password during the first setup.

Run:

```bash
sudo cat /var/lib/jenkins/secrets/initialAdminPassword
```

Copy the password and enter it into the Jenkins web interface.

---

## 8. Jenkins Initial Configuration

After entering the password:

1. Select **Install suggested plugins**
2. Create the Jenkins administrator user
3. Configure the Jenkins URL
4. Finish the setup

---

## 9. Useful Jenkins Commands

### Check Jenkins status

```bash
sudo systemctl status jenkins
```

### Start Jenkins

```bash
sudo systemctl start jenkins
```

### Stop Jenkins

```bash
sudo systemctl stop jenkins
```

### Restart Jenkins

```bash
sudo systemctl restart jenkins
```

### Enable Jenkins at boot

```bash
sudo systemctl enable jenkins
```

### View Jenkins logs

```bash
sudo journalctl -u jenkins
```

Follow live logs:

```bash
sudo journalctl -u jenkins -f
```

---

## 10. Jenkins Directory

Jenkins stores its main data under:

```text
/var/lib/jenkins
```

Important directory:

```text
/var/lib/jenkins/workspace
```

Jenkins jobs use workspace directories to clone repositories and execute builds.

---

## 11. Verify Jenkins Port

Check whether Jenkins is listening on port `8080`:

```bash
sudo ss -lntp | grep 8080
```

You should see Jenkins listening on port `8080`.

